README
======

This suite runs each case/<name>.ri through the cgil translator for the host
language encoded in the file name -- the field between the first and second
'-' -- and compares the result against the expected file beside it, which
carries the language as its extension. So case/test-go-02.ri is translated by
cgil-go and checked against case/test-go-02.go.

A case/<name>.args is a run that must fail. The translator, named in the file
name the same way, is given the arguments in it, split on white space, and none
if it is empty. Its standard input is case/<name>.in, if there is one, so a
case can give it an input file as /dev/stdin. It must exit with 1, and print
case/<name>.err on standard error. These run from the suite's build directory.

The translators are the ones built from src/cgil, the live sources that ragel's
host backends share. This suite is what holds them to their output: the cases
and the expected text are fixed, so a difference in what comes out is a change
in cgil. There are 29 translation cases over the twelve languages, and 6
failing runs of cgil-c, which check its usage line and the errors it reports.

The rlhc.d suite looks similar and measures something else. Its translators are
a frozen 2019 copy of these, kept so that colm is the only moving part; see the
README there. That one tests colm, this one tests cgil.

The translator writes to a file rather than to standard output, so the harness
gives it /dev/stdout and compares in memory. Nothing is written beside the
sources.
